            <ArticleTitle>Coronary muscular bridge mimicking acute stent thrombosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e16-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5830/CVJA-2011-005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A patient who develops hypotension or angina pectoris after intravenous inotropic agents should be assessed for dynamic left ventricular outflow obstruction or the presence of a muscular bridge. In this case report, we present a patient with low ejection fraction who developed hypotension and angina pectoris with inotropic therapy after coronary intervention. We performed a coronary angiogram to exclude stent thrombosis but a muscular bridge was found in the segment distal to the stent.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
